":"mathematical-premise"},"statement":"Assume AC. For 0≤r≤3 and any coherent analytic O_{P^r(C)}-module F, every H^q(P^r(C),F), q≥0, is finite-dimensional. With the locally defined coherent-section Fréchet topology and the induced Čech quotient topology these cohomology groups are Hausdorff. The proof includes the required Fréchet open-mapping and Schwartz compact-complex finiteness results, local coherent-section topology, and compactness of restriction; none is presumed to follow from a Banach-only or one-variable theorem.","strategy":"Develop metrizable complete locally convex section spaces and prove the Fréchet open-mapping theorem by Baire and successive approximation; Banach open mapping is only an analogy. Prove Schwartz compact-perturbation and its compact morphism of Fréchet complexes finite-dimensional/Hausdorff cohomology corollary. Define coherent section topology as a closed-relation quotient and show chart/generator independence using Artin–Rees/Krull. Prove several-variable Montel from polydisc Cauchy estimates, Ascoli and diagonal compact exhaustion (the imported named Montel theorem is plane-only). Prove compact coherent restriction maps. Construct finite nested Leray covers of P^r subordinate to distinguished resolution neighborhoods and justify all finite-intersection acyclicity. If intersections are not coordinate polydiscs, explicitly prove their pseudoconvex chart geometry and scalar Dolbeault vanishing, then coherent dimension shifting; do not apply pair3 to arbitrary intersections. Apply the proved compact-complex theorem to the resulting restriction of Čech complexes. This contains substantial internal prerequisites and remains unproved.","proof_strategy":"direct","axiom_base":"ZF + full AC; record resolutions, countable shrinking/diagonal choices, Baire/Fréchet arguments and supplier-inherited choice uses precisely. No physical premise.","provenance":{"statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-supplied"},"sources":{"references":[{"title